# 9. Identify Matched and Unmatched Records

### Requirement

Show every employee and every department and identify whether they have a match.

```sql
SELECT 
    E.EmployeeID,
    E.EmployeeName,
    D.DepartmentID,
    D.DepartmentName,
    CASE
        WHEN E.EmployeeID IS NULL 
            THEN 'Department has no employee'
        WHEN D.DepartmentID IS NULL 
            THEN 'Employee has no department'
        ELSE 'Matched'
    END AS MatchStatus
FROM Employees E
FULL OUTER JOIN Departments D
    ON E.DepartmentID = D.DepartmentID;
```

### Example Result

| EmployeeID | EmployeeName | DepartmentID | DepartmentName | MatchStatus                |
| ---------- | ------------ | ------------ | -------------- | -------------------------- |
| 101        | John         | 1            | HR             | Matched                    |
| 102        | Priya        | 2            | IT             | Matched                    |
| 103        | David        | 2            | IT             | Matched                    |
| NULL       | NULL         | 3            | Finance        | Department has no employee |
| NULL       | NULL         | 4            | Sales          | Department has no employee |
| 104        | Sarah        | NULL         | NULL           | Employee has no department |

### Why use CASE?

We want to **keep all records** and label their status instead of filtering them out.

